**filter() accepts Predicate<? super T> — the wildcard:**

```java
// Predicate<Trade>:
stream.filter(trade -> trade.isSettled())          // lambda
stream.filter(Trade::isSettled)                    // method ref

// Predicate<? super Trade> — accepts parent types too:
Predicate<Object> notNull = Objects::nonNull;      // Predicate<Object>
stream.filter(notNull);                            // OK! Object is super of Trade

// Composition:
Predicate<Trade> settled = Trade::isSettled;
Predicate<Trade> large = t -> t.getAmount().compareTo(threshold) > 0;
stream.filter(settled.and(large))                  // AND
stream.filter(settled.or(large))                   // OR
stream.filter(settled.negate())                    // NOT
stream.filter(Predicate.not(Trade::isSettled))     // static NOT (Java 11)
```

**Internal mechanics — how filter() REALLY works:**

```java
// When you call:
Stream<Trade> filtered = trades.stream().filter(Trade::isSettled);
// Java creates a ReferencePipeline$StatelessOp that wraps the source.
// NO filtering happens yet!

// Simplified internal pseudocode (from OpenJDK ReferencePipeline):
// @Override
// Sink<Trade> opWrapSink(Sink<Trade> downstream) {
//     return new Sink<Trade>() {
//         @Override
//         public void accept(Trade t) {
//             if (predicate.test(t)) {     // THE ACTUAL FILTER CHECK
//                 downstream.accept(t);     // pass to next stage
//             }
//             // if false → element is simply NOT passed → dropped
//         }
//     };
// }
```

**Spliterator — the engine under the hood:**

```java
// Spliterator<T> is the low-level iterator that drives stream traversal.
// Three key methods:

// 1. tryAdvance(Consumer) — pull ONE element
spliterator.tryAdvance(element -> {
    if (predicate.test(element)) downstream.accept(element);
});
// Returns true if element was available, false if exhausted

// 2. forEachRemaining(Consumer) — pull ALL remaining
spliterator.forEachRemaining(element -> {
    if (predicate.test(element)) downstream.accept(element);
});

// 3. trySplit() — divide for parallel processing
Spliterator<Trade> left = spliterator.trySplit();
// If non-null: left handles first half, original handles second half
// Each half can be processed by a different ForkJoinPool thread
```

**Why filter is stateless:**

filter does NOT buffer elements. It does NOT need to see future elements to decide about the current one. Each element is independently tested: `predicate.test(element)` → true/false. This means filter works on infinite streams and uses O(1) extra memory.

